  "slug": "sequences-promoter-38cf",
  "summary": "Structure-based ligand modeling depends on pose and complex geometry that predicted models may not reliably capture.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the evaluation of structural models used for protein-glycan binding and membrane complex assessment. CARVE provides a glycan-specific framework that scores binding-pose quality with oRMSD, irRMSD and Fnat under DP-scaled thresholds, benchmarked on 1,974 complexes. A separate audit of roughly 189k AlphaFold-Multimer transmembrane complexes in the AFDB checks membrane compatibility, finding most have plausible bilayer geometry while identifying specific limitations. The NCF1 study uses AlphaFold to support tail-mediated autoinhibition, placing it at the edge of this thread. What is new is the shift from asking whether a predicted structure exists to asking whether its geometry is fit for downstream modeling. The studies report no head-to-head accuracy comparisons across methods, so the contribution lies in metrics and evaluation design rather than demonstrated superiority.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
